.endsect
.newsect Introduction
.!
Object oriented programming is now coming into acceptance in
major computing circles.
Software houses such as Microsoft and Borland International
are finally offering ${oo} Pascal,
years after Apple first made available Niklaus Wirth's Objective-Pascal
for the Macintosh system.
AT&T now offers an ${oo} version of "C" called C++ [Stro86].
The NeXT computer has been designed around Objective-C version 4.
Major companies are committing themselves to this new challange.
.!
  The computers of today are finally overcoming many of the "drawbacks"
that have long putoff serious consideration
of an ${oo} system [Cox86].
Large memories, fast disks, graphic diplays, and capable processors are
today reaching a financially feasible level to make the ideas of
${oo} possible for the masses.
.!
.!
  This programming language is an attempt at hybriding a medium
level language with ${oo} constructs.
The goal is to provide flexibility with classical (?) ${oo}
programming layout (ala Smalltalk).
The translator accepts the hybrid language and ensures
its output is complete, legal "C" code.
The translator system is model after Objective-C as presented
by Dr. Cox in [Cox86].
This author's particular system is currently in the public domain.
.!
  The alpha releases of ${shname} started in August 1989.
The first Amiga "release" was January 1990,
along with the Xenix/386.
The planned rollout schedule is as follows.
An MS-DOS version should appear after the summer of 1990.
A second Amiga version should also appear about this time,
and will be released to Fred Fish.
.!
.!
.sect 1 1 Why?
.!
   Initially this system is destined for very limited release as
much of the knowledge here came from the cookbook of [Cox86].
Dr. Cox provided the recipe and I merely followed his directions therein.
I have legal concerns about distributing the method code that's
been typed in, nearly verbatim, out of [Cox86].
His coding style is rather distasteful (his being very text-space conscious),
and I've tried to correct some stylistic differences.
Since there is little an ${oo} system can do without support classes (like 
.class Array
and
.class Set)
, these classes have to be included somehow.
Most of the syntax is version 4
with classes and methods from version 2.
But the system is moving towards the version 4 class ${heir}.
.!
.!
.need 5
.underline all
.underline
  Why am I doing this?
.underline words
My personal favorite for object oriented systems is
Eiffel by Bertrand Meyer [Meyer88]: ${agc} is a must,
and static method checking increases program reliability.
(But a 180K executable just for "hello world" is little excessive!)
This system, on the other hand, is rather crude:
no garabage collection, opaque error messages,
and a design that encourages bypassing
the data hiding rules of ${oo} systems.
However, this system provides a good feel for the paradigms
in an object based system.
Thus, the basic ideas can be exercised and demonstration systems built.
Perhaps a few ideas can be spawned with this system,
and a dream become reality...
The author's personal interest is in operating systems,
and someday he'd like to construct a layered, ${oo} operating system
based on the concepts presented here.
.!
.need 5
  Also noteworthy is the "commercial viability"
of entering the Amiga market.
Since there are not tens of millions of installed machines,
small companies probably see the Amiga market as
"not worth their time."
As such, the products Amiga owners want just have to be written
by Amiga programmers instead of waiting for a vendor to sell the
application.
(Fred Fish was very insightful about the need for public domain
software for the Amiga.)
For the vendors, supporting different platforms may be too much
overhead for them to cost-effectively manage.
As a side note,
in Novemeber 1989 Dataquest estimated that after 2-1/2 years
OS/2 had an installed base of 180,000 units.
(I don't know the percentage of these OS/2 systems that were
"promotion packages.")
Would you write software for this small base?
By this date, Commodore-Amiga had shipped over a million total of
the A500, A1000 and A2000 models,
all running the same multi-tasking operating system.
.!
.!
.need 6
.underline all
.underline
  Why is source code being distributed?
.underline words
Someone may want
things setup just a little different.
There are also many lessons to be learned from creating ${oo} systems.
Do you think adding ${agc} would be easy?
Well, try it and find out for yourself!
Initially, the translator was mostly a
.program bison
and
.program flex
grammar with a little glue code from me.
Now, only the FSF's scanner is used.
As a *big* "thank you, I want to return the favor"
to Stallman and Fred Fish,
source code is graciously included.
.!
.!
.need 8
.sect 1 2 Background
  I've enjoyed the excellent material available on ${oo} techniques.
The 1981 August Byte issue on Smalltalk-80, and Dr. Cox [Cox86],
were first to change my thinking.
Books about Smalltalk [Budd87]
have also given me insight to the implementation difficulties involved.
The first section of [Meyer88] fuels the object-drive paradigm
and shows how top-down programming fails for non-trival projects.
.!

