.! ---------------------
.!
.newsect System Overview
.!
The ${shname} system is a collection of programs and runtime libraries.
Header files relating to the classes are also required for any
compilation.
This system extends the C language [Kern78]
instead of creating a completely different language.
.!
.sect 1 1 Kind-of Object Oriented
  Following the lead in [Cox86], ${shname} is an object oriented
preprocessor for the "C" language.
For the programmer, the system provides single inheritance.
The root class is named
.class Object [Gold89].
This class is mandatory for any ${shname} application;
all other classes are pulled in as required by your application.
Allocated objects are of the single type "id",
and any object reference may be assigned to any other object referring
variable.
The future reveals that typed object references will be introduced,
and will co-exist with untyped object references.
.!
    Method selectors are bound dynamically at run-time.
Any method may be sent to any object; successful understanding is
another matter.
Methods are generally public unless you don't mention them
in the public interface file.
Custom places an underscore (_) in front of private methods.
Instance variables are hidden from the application, but not
completely unreachable by it.
.!
.!
.sect 1 2 Product Compatibility
  There are a few differences between the ${lgname} - ${shname} system
and Objective-C version 4 from StepStone, Inc.
The preprocessor directive #import is not implemented.
As long as ${shaname} uses an outboard preprocessor, this directive
can't be implemented.
Use #include instead; you'll get a few warnings, though.
My base classes aren't as complete, either.
Although I'm moving towards the verison classes, their implementation
may be a little lacking.
And remember, there is no graphical user interface.
.!
.!
.sect 1 3 System Components
   A running ${shname} application is the product of
source files, interface definitions, and translation programs.
The programs include an ${shname} to "C" source code translator,
a class interface helper,
and a pre-linker utility.
The preprocessor,
.program cpp
is included for sites that
don't have this program separate from the "C" compiler itself.
Under Xenix, the preprocessor is part of the compiler's first pass,
which does preliminary syntax checking.
It will choke on the extended syntax.
Your site need only contribute a K&R "C" compiler [Kern78].
.! Prototyping and constant types are due for the second release.
The interface definition file includes information about the
class, its superclass, and method return types.
.!
.!
.!
   In addition to the translator there are a few support programs
included in the ${product} package.
A prelinker program arranges for the needed classes to be included
from the ${shname} class library.
Since the translator's scanner was built with
.program flex,
it's included, too.
After changing two lines of
.filename scan.l,
the input is acceptable to UNIX
.program lex.
Programs enough to recompile the system are included also.
.!
.need 3
   At present (April 1990), the system is remarkably independent of
the actual machine it's running on.
.! Calling the host system's object library maintance program from the
.! .filename makefile
.! is as specific as it gets.
Calling vsprintf(), or something similar,
is the height of weirdness for the runtime.
Applications can be generated with 16-bit or 32-bit int's.
The translator relies strictly on the standard "C" runtime library,
not any Amiga or UNIX specific routines.
The translator survives in 64K code and data segments,
but not in a combined 64K segment for code
.underline
and
data.
.!
.endsect
.!
.! ---------------------
.!
.newsect What the Translator Does
.!
The translator handles one class at a time.
The definition of a class is split between
the public interface definition header file and the
private class implementation code file.
(The totality of methods a class responds to includes those
of its superclasses, also.)
The public interface defines the class's instance variables and
methods that it specifically implements.
(See also C++ in [Stro86].)
The implementation file holds source code to implement the
methods, and it can include any private methods to
assist the class's objects in carrying out their work.
.!
   Before compiling any class, information about the class's
single superclass must be available.
This superclass is used to link the class into the class ${heir}.
Instance variables are collected by reading in the interface
definition file for all the superclasses.
.!
  A implementation difficulty for ${oo} systems is translating the
${mss} into a "universally unique code" for
the particular collection of classes that are needed to form
the application.
The method choosen by Cox [Cox86] is for each phylum (grouping of classes)
to maintain
an array of codes representing the ${mss} unique to that
phylum, ie, those not defined in its super-${phs}.
(Phylum is a collection of related classes,
such as Foundation, Collection, or Windowing.)
Values from this array are read out and passed
as the message selector code.
At startup-time, each phylum's array is adjusted by a scalar representing
the count of methods already defined and adjusted in the phylum
before it.
This has the advantage that each phylum
can be built and compiled separatedly and without knowledge of
other phylums.
The cost is small at startup-time, and a single indirection during
message invocation (array fetch at a fixed offset).
.!
   Once the program is ready to be linked, Cox's runtime
substrate constructs a single list of classes.
This list is used to preset all classes initially at startup-time,
ie, [Sequence initialize].
A single list of method selector names is also constructed..
This list is used primarily for run-time error reporting
(ie, "error: 'ByteArray' doesn't respond to [display:rop:]").
These lists are built by the
.program prelink
, compiled,
and passed to the system's linker along with the other module names.
.!
   For ${shname}, the basics of the above system have been implemented.
The difference is that each object module includes a list of
methods it references (phylums are not used).
The
.program prelink
program builds a list of modules required for the application.
When the runtime system initializes itself,
it walks this list finding all the dependent classes.
From this list, all the methods are given codes, and every class
initialized.
.!
.!
.sect 1 1 Language Extensions
  To support the message expression, a grammar extension is created.
Several "frills" (translator directives)
have also been added for convience sakes.
Translator directives are defined
for access to the internal information
of the translator.
All files destined for the translator must somehow contained a
.!  A non-break space would be great here!  *******
.filename _#include
.filename <objc/objc.h>
directive.
If you're defining a new class, this is taken care of for you already
by the Object class interface file.
If the file doesn't define any classes, then the directive
"@includes()" must be placed after the file's list of include files
(see
.filename user.m
in the directory
.filename ooc/methods
for details).
.!
   After
.program cpp
has processed the code, it's the translator's turn.
First, all comments are stripped (again).
If
.program cpp
doesn't strip them out, then ${shname} will.
An additional comment form beginning with "//"
and continuing until the end of the line is also recognized [Stro86].
Right now there is no way to preserve comments, so
.program lint
isn't too effective here.
(This may change with an integrated
.program cpp
preprocessor.)
.!
   Translator directives, beginning with "@",
allow the programmer to access information in the classes'
dataset without having to "hardwire" in such information.
One directive is "@selector()" with takes a method selector
and returns
.! a function that returns the
an implementation defined selector code (type `sel').
If you need to get a selector from an arbitrary string,
use the substrate function sel__getUid().
The directive will promote to va-dcl methods (see below);
the runtime function won't.
The directive "@defs()" accepts a class name and returns the
instance variable declaration.
The returned text is suitable for inclusion in a structure definition
(see below).
.!
.need 14
.opencode
#include <objc/substrate.h>

//  This is a new comment!
id  ignoreMe()
{
   struct { @defs(View) short xxx; }  cheater;
   extern char   *users_method;

   [obj perform:@selector(titleStr:) with:" Title "];
   return sel_getUid( strcat( users_method, "vaDcl:" ) );
}
.endcode
.!
  For files that don't define any classes but use message passing,
there should be a "@includes()" directive after the file's normal
list of include files.
.!
.!
   The message expression is the primary reason the translator was built.
Messages begin with a left square bracket (_[)
and close with a right square bracket (_]).
Between the square brackets is the message target,
and some combination of selectors and arguments expressions,
seperated by spaces (except va-dcl, see below).
The smallest message expression
is a target and method selector like "new" or "hash".
The method may also take arguments, which are separated from the
selector by a colon (_:), ie "at:_ 1" and "put:_ target".
This syntax is similar to a "code block" in Smalltalk.
.!
  Some messages allow a variable number of arguments to follow
the fixed selectors.
These arguments are unlabeled and are separated by commas (_,).
The translator will automatically append "vaDcl:" to the method name
internally, thus the last statement below invokes the message "with:vaDcl:".
If a method name is unknown, another search with "vaDcl:" appended
is tried.
This way, "vaDcl:" methods may take a single parameter
(after the opening name) and not require a trailing dummy parameter
(see last statement in sample below).
This is called auto-detection of "vaDcl:" type methods.
.!
.opencode 12
#include <objc/Sequence.h>
#include <objc/Array.h>
#include <objc/PointXY.h>
@includes

//  Just a sample...
void my_test()
{
   id   obj, targ, dest;
   int  x_coord, y_coord;

   targ = [Sequence new];      /* No arguments */
   dest = [target find:nil];   /* One arg */
   obj  = [[Array new:3] at:1 put:target];   /* Two args */
   targ = [PointXY x: x_coord y: y_coord ];  /* Two args */
   [targ setXY: 90 : 40 ];
   dest = [Sequence with:2, target, nil];  /* Many va-dcl's */
   targ = [self error: "It's OK" ];     /* 1 parm va-dcl */
}
.!
.endcode
.figure Application Using Objects
.!
.br
The fourth line (reference to class
.class PointXY_)
invokes the factory method "x:y:".
The statement after it invokes the method "setXY::".
The second argument isn't a va-dcl method type, it's just unlabeled.
Sometimes this style is handy, but usually it adds confusion.
.!
.!
.sect 1 2 After Translation
.!
  A prelinker is used to build a list of classes
that will be brought together to form the final application.
A small
.filename .c
file is built, which must be compiled and added into
the list of object modules for the appliction.
This list tells the linker which classes to extract from
the library.
Any dependencies of the included classes are pulled in by their own
external class references.
It's also used by the substrate at startup-time to build
the central list of method selector codes and class names.
The list is searched depth-first to find classes that are used by
classes that are used by the classes that the application requests.
.!
.!
  Normally, this small file need only be rebuilt when the
list of modules the user provides changes in some way,
or a new class is added to the application.
See the
.filename makefile's
for sample dependencies for rebuilding this file.
.!
.!
.! ---------------------
.!
.endsect
.newsect Translation
.!
Translation is accomplished in four phases.
Source starts as
.filename .m
files (methods), are translated to
.filename .c
files, and then compiled to object files.
The system linker, using the prelinker's output,
coagulates the user and system classes creating the user's application.
.!
  The first two passes are driven by ${shname}, which calls
.program cpp
first.
The translator then converts class definitions and methods
into legal "C" code.
The translator uses information in the @interface clauses
to relate the external classes.
The system's "C" compiler runs next.
The prelinker creates a list of classes for use by the substrate.
Finally the system's linker/loader pulls together object and library
modules.
.!
.!
   The ${shname} translator first uses
.program cpp
to process the input source file, stripping comments,
inserting included files, and choosing conditional code.
If the source code depends on the "C" compiler
defining certain symbols relating to specific options, like
.boldon
__LARGE__DATA__MODEL,
.bold off
then these define's must be given explicitly to
.program ${progname}
to pass to
.program cpp
(see "Translator Gotchas!" below).
The processed text is stored into a temporary file.
.!
   The translator reads this temporary file,
translating it, and delivering a
.filename .c
output file.
Headers for the methods and a dictionary describing the methods
is built,
and the file is ready for the "C" compiler.
A little preprocessing is again needed
since symbols are declared during translation.
The translator will include
.filename <objc/objc.h>
a second time to clean up after itself.
The include command is triggered either by a class @implementation
clause or the @includes() directive.
By including
.filename <objc/objc.h>
after the translator has run,
the translator can be decoupled from the actual implementation,
allowing cross-compiling simply by changing this include file.
The "C" compiler takes over for conversion to object code.
.!
  Before the application can be linked, a list of needed classes
must be constructed.
The list is used to initialize all the classes
when the application is executed.
The
.program prelink
program scans the list of classes and files
the user in contributing to the application.
These filenames are combined with names from the "must init" file.
.!
.!
.figopen 6 44
                        ________
                       /        \
                      |\________/|
                      |   user   |
                      |  source  |
                       \________/
                           |
    ________               V
   /        \        +------------+
  |\________/| <---- |   O.O.C.   | ----> +-------+
  | i/f file | ----> | Translator | <---- |  cpp  |
   \________/        +------------+       +-------+
                           |
                           V
                     +------------+
      .              |     C      |
      :              |  compiler  |
      |              +------------+
  ____V____                |
 /         \            ___V____             _________
|\_________/|          /        \           /         \
|   user    |         |\________/|         |\_________/|
|  classes  |         |  object  |         | Substrate |
 \_________/           \________/           \_________/
      \                    |                    /
       \                   V                   /
        \            +------------+           /
         `---------> | Pre-Linker |          /
                     +------------+         /
                     |  Linker &  | <------'
                     | Sys Librs  |
                     +------------+
                           |
