                    |\___________/|
                    | Application |
                     \___________/
.figclose
.figure Data flow in ${shname}
.!
.!
.sect 1 1 Translator Gotchas!
.!
   Discussed here are a few points to keep in mind when using the
${lgname}.
The warnings here are mostly the result of certain assumptions to
simplify the translator a bit,
and will probably change as the translator evolves.
These idiosyncracies will probably return errors from the "C" compiler
instead of compiling incorrect code.
But if your application is amiss,
you should first suspect the generated code.
.!
.! ____________________________  cpp
.!
  The first idiosyncracy concerns the use of
.program cpp
to first treat the code.  Since it doesn't know about "//" comments,
the comment text has to obey certain rules;
rules like matching quote marks.
So for a line like: "// don't look now!",
.program cpp
will complain about "unterminated string" on the line
.underline
after
the "//" comment.
What happens is
.program cpp
is tripping over a single quote (_') in the comment line.
There is no matching single quote on the line.
If the error line is a comment, or just after a comment line,
just ignore this error message.
Also, since your host compiler didn't invoke
.program cpp
, there is no way symbols relating to the program's memory model can be set.
These defines can passed
using the -p or -d options of ${shname},
or more permanetly set using environment variables.
.!
.! ____________________________  cpp
.!
.!
  Among high-level programming languages, monadic operators that
trail the expression are rare, except in "C" [Kern78].
The usual handling cares should be applied to auto-increment
and auto-decrement operators.
Witness the famous:
"a++ + ++b."
Inserting a few parenthesis here will only make the code more
understandable.
If you forget the parenthesis, the post-inc and post-dec will
associate right to left, in accordance with K&R [Kern78].
This code sample points out a few concerns.
.!
.opencode 11
- foo
{
   id   *there, cls = [super class];

   there = (id *) IV(self);
   while( *there )
      [(*there++) free];

   if( [self hash] > 5 )
      cls = [self class];
   (there-1)[1] = nil;         /* array store */
}  /* - foo */
.endcode
.!

Because the translator simply inspects the tokens as they appear,
and not really following what they mean, it can get
confused at times.
How one token relates to its neighbors isn't inherit in the parsing.
.!** Parsing if() and while() statments can cause this.
.!** Most of the time, it shouldn't bother anything, but if
.!** there are warnings, a set of curly braces (_{_})
.!** might be needed after the if() or while().
.!
   The above line marked "array store" is an example of where a closing
parenthesis (_)) would be next to an open square bracket (_[).
The translator assumes this really is an array store
(since the syntax is legal and correct).
rather than going for the message expression.
Note, if a function call were added to the beginning of the line,
(ie, "foo(there)[1] = nil;")
the square bracket (_[) might be mistaken as beginning a message.
.!
  In these cases, the translator doesn't treat the expression
as a message expression.
If the translator thought wrong, the "C" compiler should (hopefully)
catch the message translation as illegal "C" syntax,
and display an compiler error message.
Remember, the translator is conservative in its decisions,
and the highway to Hell is paved with BMW's.
.!
.!
.endsect
.! ----------------------
.!
.newsect The Reality of Runtime
.!
Before the user application can begin running,
the system must initialize itself.
Already the list of classes has been created.
Each class will see the "initialize" message first,
which will most likely be fielded by class
.class Object
as the default implementation (inheritance in action).
The user's main() function name is been changed via a #define in 
.filename <objc/objc.h>
to app_main(), and exit() is changed to app_exit().
This allows (almost) seamless operation
with pre-existing source files, and requires nothing special from
the run-time environment.
.!
.!
.sect 1 1 Method Selector Codes
  Before an application can run, all the method selector codes must
be assigned unique codes.
Instead of using string names, the dispatcher routine __msg()
uses these codes to look up the method to run.
Somehow the message "at:put:" has to be different from "perform:with:",
and yet all occurances of "at:put:" must be the same.
String equivalents of each message must be stored for run-time
error messages (ie, "error: `ByteArray' doesn't respond to [titleStr:]").
.!
.!
   The selector codes for each class are stored in array.
Eventually the application starts up and initializes itself.
As each class in the application is first discovered by the
substrate startup code, its methods are assigned codes.
The selector codes don't discriminate between factory or instance methods.
The substrate holds a list of method string names and codes.
This list can be consulted at run-time using the functions
(defined in
.filename <objc/substrate.h>
):
.!
.opencode
 /*  Convert a method string into SEL code:   */
extern  SEL   sel_getUid PARMS(( CONST STR colon_name ));

 /* convert an internal SEL ID into a string: */
extern 	STR   sel_getName PARMS(( SEL sel_id ));
.br
.endcode
.br
.!
.!
.!
.sect 1 2 Returned Values
  Return values from the message invocation are a little sticky to
deal with properly.
The message expressions are translated to "C" functions calls of the form:
.!
.need 5
.opencode
   extern id  _msg( );
{
   /*  Was [array at:ndx put:store_obj]  */
   _msg( array, /*at:put:*/_ocSAobject[4], ndx, store_obj );
}
.endcode
.br
.!
Somehow the return value of the message must be coerced into
the proper return type of that method (as stated in the class
interface definition file).
.!
  Cox [Cox86] on page 86 points out a way to get
around most of the difficulties with typecasting a return value.
So instead of typecasting just the return value,
he proposes changing the type of the identifier __msg()
into an identifier that's a function returning the
appropriate return type for the specific method being invoked.
Although the syntax looks ugly, the translator and the compiler are
the only ones that see it (but you can see it below).
This funkiness allows returning of structures and complex data types.
.!
  This makes
.program lint
happy since the returned type matches the type of the variable
the result is stored into.
Many methods are used more for their side effects than their return values.
But since __msg() always returns a value,
and it's generally an "id" type,
.program lint
will complain that many results go unused.
.!
.!  FIX:  change return type into 'double'   ****
NOTE: right now, don't return anything larger (bit-wise)
than a `void *'.
Since the return type of __msg() is `void *', and since the
method return isn't directly from the method code back to the
caller, part of the return value can get lost (truncated).
.!  FIX:  change return type into 'double'   ****
.!
.need 8
.opencode
{
   VECTOR    vect;

   vect = (*((VECTOR (*)()) _msg))
               ( mouse, /* point */ _ocSApointxy[2] );
}
.endcode
.!
.!
   The handling of variable-count arguments to an object is taken
care of for you by the translator.
As the translator is evaluating each parameter in the selector area,
it watches for a comma.  Seeing one, it switches to "va-dcl" mode.
First the translator appends "vaDcl:" to the method selector it's
building, then it simply passes along the remaining parameters to
the output file.
Because the translator doesn't come out of "va-dcl" mode until
the message is complete (closing "_]" seen);
there can be no named parameters afterwards.
There is also an auto-detection of "vaDcl:" methods (described above).
.!
  The third statement below is another possibility for "vaDcl:" methods.
It's of bad form and should really be split up into two functions.
The second method gives an extreme example of
what can be done with "vaDcl:" mode.
.!
.opencode 11
.!
void create_three()
{
   id   a, b, c;

       /* Invoke the method with:vaDcl: */
   a = [Array with:3, item1, item2, item3];        /* Good */
       /* Invoke the method titleStr:vaDcl: */
   b = [Menu titleStr:"X" with:2, "Save","Quit"];  /* OK.. */
       /* Invoke the method with:vaDcl: */
   c = [Datagram with:2, "joe", "al" msg:a];     /* ERROR! */
}

.need 10
//	Not logical, Sir.  Your index is out of range:
- boundsViolation: (register int) anOffset
{
   return( [self error: capacity > 0
         ? "bounds violation: %d outside range [0..%d]",
         : "zero capacity array", anOffset, capacity-1] );
}
.br
.!
.endcode
.figure "va-dcl" method promotion
.br
.!
.!
.sect 1 3 Application Exit
  Since many microcomputer "C" runtime libraries
don't have _atexit() (yet),
the include file redirects exit().
This allows the substrate to cleanup.
The substrate then calls the classes' "at exit" handler in FIFO order
of how they were added.
Currently, there is room for
the number of classes in the application minus one handlers.
Setting the global (BOOL) _msgTrace will print out some statistics
from the message dispatcher.
when the application exits.
The include file
.filename <objc/substrate.h>
hold a definition for this variable.
.!
.!
.endsect
.!
.! ---------------------
.newsect Theory of Methods and Factories
.!
This section describes the run-time environment the objects exist within,
and the "virtual reality" for the application.
.!
.figname cim +1
.!
.figopen 0 36
.bold
 Objects             Instance Base             Factory Base
                                          ,_________________________,
                                          |                         |
               ,------------------------------------------------,   |
               |                          |                     |   |
+--------+     V    +--------------+      V   +--------------+  |   |
| isa   ----------> | isa         ----------> | isa         ------->|
+========+     ^    +--------------+      ^   +--------------+  |   |
               |    | super     --> Nil   |   | super       ----+   |
               |    | "Object"     |      |   | "_Object"    |      |
               |    | size       4 |      |   | size      24 |      |
               |    +--------------+      |   +--------------+      |
               |                          |                         |
               `----------------------,   `---------------------,   |
                                      |                         |   |
+--------+          +--------------+  |       +--------------+  |   |
| isa   ----------> | isa         ----------> | isa         ------->|
+========+     ^    +--------------+  |   ^   +--------------+  |   |
|        |     |    | super       ----'   |   | super       ----'   |
+--------+     |    | "View"       |      |   | "_View"      |      |
               |    | size      20 |      |   | size      24 |      |
               |    +--------------+      |   +--------------+      |
               |                          |                         |
               `----------------------,   `---------------------,   |
                                      |                         |   |
+--------+          +--------------+  |       +--------------+  |   |
| isa   ----------> | isa         ----------> | isa         ------->|
|        |          +--------------+  |       +--------------+  |
+--------+          | super       ----'       | super       ----'
|        |          | "Fixture"    |          | "_Fixture"   |
+========+          | size      26 |          | size      24 | 
|        |          +--------------+          +--------------+
+--------+
.figclose
.figure Classes in Memory
.!
.!
.sect 1 1 Factories
  Software factories create software objects.
These objects carry pointers to structures that contain
information about how much space
the object occupies and which methods it responds to.
In a factory method, "self" refers to the factory object.
It is a actual parameter (along with "__cmd" which is the method selector)
declared as part of the function definition.
"self" can be reassigned to a new instance of itself and
returned from a factory method, just as in [Cox86].
However, this practice is discouraged by the author.
Factory objects can never be free'd.
.!
.!
.sect 1 2 Instances
  Once created ([new] method),
objects can be manipulated and assigned at will.
The caller must track memory usage since there is
no ${agc} nor referencing counting.
This does place an extra burden on the programmer, but all of us
learned Pascal in order to better discipline ourselves...
.!
  There is a facility to provide cleanup processing for classes.
At any time, a method may declare an "at exit" handler.
A function that takes no parameters and returns no specific value
will be called when the application is finished, just before
the message dispatcher is shutdown.
If a class is responsible for managing a system resource,
this routine might check that the resource was indeed allocated,
and then return it back to the system.
The strange code below sets up an "at exit" handler
The first `if' statement in the method +initialize
is passed the message to the superclass
if the init'ing classs isn't
.class Window.
We can't reference 'self' here, since we're not
.class Window;
we're one of its decendent classes.
.!
.!
.opencode 31
.!
#include "window.h"
#include <objc/substrate.h>

short            ndx_win_opens;      /* Track window opens */
struct Window   *win_opens[ 10 ];    /* Stack of windows opened */

//  Cleanup routine for Window class. Close any outstanding windows:
static void free_win_list()
{
   int    j;

   for( j=ndx_win_opens ; --j >= 0 ; ) {
      if( win_opens[j] != NULL )
         CloseWindow( win_opens[j] );
   }
}   /* free_win_list */

+ initialize
{
   /*  ONLY class Window may pass, others continue the search: */
   if( self != &instWindow )
      return [instWindow.clsSuper initialize] ;   /* cannot be 'self' */

   if( !(self->isa->sysFlags & OC_SF_INIT_DONE) ) {
      printf( "[Window init]\n" );
      self->isa->sysFlags |= OC_SF_INIT_DONE;
      _oc_atExit( free_win_list );    /* Catch errors */

      /*  Allocate resources here.. */
      ndx_win_opens = 0;
   }
   return( (id) self );
}   /* + initialize */
.!
.endcode
.figure Classs Initialization, and Cleanup Hook
.!
.!
.sect 1 3 Methods
   In each method function there is a variable called "self".
It relates to
the ${recr} of the message of the invocation statement that
called it.
If code in class
.class Object
is executing, "self" could be
.class
IdArray.
The second variable the translator always creates is "__cmd",
which is the method selector code for the current method.
The types are "id" (for factories a pointer to struct __SHARED;
for instance methods a pointer to struct __PRIVATE)
and "SEL" (usually a 32-bit int), respectively.
.!
.figname figiv +1
   The translator tracks references to your class's instance variables
for you,
so the "self->" qualifier for instance variables can be omitted.
However, if you are referencing an object defined local to a method,
its instance variables must be explicitly referenced
(as you would expect, but see ${figiv}).
Also, instance variables of any parent classes must be explicitly
referenced with "self->" (but please restrain yourself).
In the code sample below,
we create a copy of ourselves, and then adjust some thresholds
based on our "self" values.
With these new settings,
the actual work is performed by a locally held copy
of ourselvses.
The result obtained is stored for a later return.
This temporary copy is then free'd and the
result is returned (${figiv} below).
.br
.opencode 18
.!
- fastSend: msg
{
   id   newMe;                /* Temp settings holder */
   id   result;               /* Result of fast send  */

   newMe = [self copy];         /* A new of ourselves */
   newMe->low_water = low_water - 1;     /* from self */
   newMe->high_water = high_water + 1;   /* from self */
   result = [newMe doIO: msg];
   [newMe free];

   return( result );
}  /* - fastSend: */
.!
.endcode
.br
.figure Referencing Instance Variables
.!
.br
   In the final statement in ${figiv},
the temporary object if free'd.
Basically, just remember to free a function's temporary
objects when you're done with them.
There are no automatic constructor or destructor invokations like in C++.
The free method will zero out the object's "isa" pointer
to catch some dangling pointer references.
But, if that memory location is reused for another object,
you won't win...
.!
.!
.sect 1 4 Dispatching
.!
   Actions performed on objects are handled by a central method
dispatcher.
Objects are sent messages by creating message expressions
that include method selectors.
Messages can also be sent to "super" (see `new' example below).
This is a ${pse}variable and is handled by the translator by invoking
a different message dispatcher.
For "super" messages,
a pointer to the current executing class is passed as an additional
parameter.
Normally the tree climb starts above the calling method's class.
For "super", it starts above the class that is currently executing
the method code.
(Cox has a technique wherein the "super" dispatcher accepts
"self" as a pointer to the class where is code is.
This pointer will be used to begin the search.
It then peeks into
its caller's stack frame to find its first parameter, which is "self".
This "self" is used as the object identifcation.
Very eechy stuff.)
.!
.figname figselect +1
.!
.opencode 33
.!
typedef struct _SHARED    *SHR;
struct  _SHARED                /*  !! Abbreviated !!  */
{
   SHR         isa;            /* Who we belong to */
   UWORD       flags;          /* System-private flags */
   SHR         clsSuper;       /* Who we inheritent from */
   char       *clsName;        /* Text name of class */
   UWORD       clsSelectSize;  /* How many methods defined */
   struct DICT *clsSelects;    /* Ptr to methods' table */
} ;

void * _msg( self, _cmd, args )
   struct _SHARED  *self;
   SEL      _cmd;
   va_args  args;
{
   struct _SHARED  *class;
   IMP             *func;

   if( self == nil )        /* nil objects are inert */
      return( nil );
   if( self->isa == nil )
      complain( "message sent to free'd object!" );

   for( class=self->isa ; class != Nil ; class=class->clsSuper )
   {
      if( (func=dict_search(class->clsSelects, _cmd)) != NULL )
         return( (*func)( self, _cmd, args, .. ) );
   }
   return [self doesNotRecognize:_cmd];     /* Error! */
}  /* _msg */
.!
.endcode
.figure Message Dispatcher
.!

The dispatcher, __msg(), uses the relations of ${cim} to
find a dictionary of method selector codes
and function (method) pointers (`IMP' pointer).
It does this by first moving to "self->isa", which always yields a
"struct __SHARED" pointer.
Inside the shared class structure is
a pointer to the selector-function dictionary.
A sequential search looks for a matching selector.
Each category of the class is searched.
If no match selector is found, then the object's superclass is consulted
by moving to "class->clsSuper", and continuing the search.
The ${pse}code outlined in ${figselect} describes the basic functions.
.!
.!
  Some performance and portabilities decisions have been
applied to the actual dispatcher (although it's still written in "C"..).
A cache of recently invoked methods helps speed the dictionary lookup.
The cache is indexed by hashing together "class" and "__cmd".
Which means that different load addresses will yield different hash values.
And allocating objects in varying sequences will affect how
long an entry survives in the cache.
.!
   In order to allow the dispatcher to be written entirely in "C",
arguments are copied from the caller's stack frame
to the ${recr} method's stack frame.
Ideally, you'd want the dispatcher to be transparent and "jump through"
into the ${recr}'s method code.
In an actual implementation, the "jump through" code
should be implemented in assembly
since the application will gain the most
from optimizing the inner loop.
.!
.!
.endsect
.! ---------------------
.!
.newsect Future Directions
Discussed below are planned additions and future possibilites
of ${product}.
Most features are discussed as if they already exist; that's just
the way they were typed in.
Some are easily done, others will require reworking
of the translator or run-time library.
They're given here for today's programmers to plan ahead and
accommodate future releases.
.!
.!
.sect 1 1 Future: Syntax
.!
  The syntax recognized by the translator is mostly
based on Objective-C version 4.
The syntax to define classes and methods in [Cox86] is outdated.
However, the classes and methods included in ${lgname}
are very much version 2.
Compatability with the present release would make ${product}
much more attractive.
The classes
.class IdArray
and
.class ByteArray
will most likely survive the updates, but the Collection group
will be moved aside in favor a more generalized "list" notion.
.!
.!
.sect 1 2 Future: Preprocessor
.!
  Another directive is "_@alias()" which assigns a method selector
to call a another method.
This aliasing is private to the class definition, as each method
selector will have its own unique selector code.
Aliasing can be used in some circumstances to save a little memory.
The protocol defined in an abstract class may default one method
to behave similar to another method, where subclass are free
to re-define either method.
The abstract class would alias the
second method to the first method.
Also, methods with multiple trailing arguments can be quickly
produced, ie "perform:", "perform:with:" and "perform:with:with:".
Here the method with most arguments would be defined, and the rest
would simply alias to that method.
In order for the processor to not crash when handling alias'ed
methods, the stack layout must be very lax.
Contrast this to machines that force static link frames,
like the Prime 50 series and IBM 370 architectures.
.!
.need 12
.opencode
#import <objc/Object.h>

_@implementation  Object ;

- perform:(SEL)sel with:obj1 with:obj2
{  ...
}

_@alias(perform:with:, perform:with:with:)
_@alias(perform:,      perform:with:with:)
.endcode
.figure @alias Example
.!
.br
.!
  Also note the directive to include the header file "import",
instead of "include".
The current version of Objective-C uses this directive to
specify that a file must be included at most once,
all other attempts are quietly ignored.
This is similar to the GNU
.program cpp
reading the "#pragma once <objc/Object.m>" directive.
.!
.!
.sect 1 3 Future: Universal Name Binding
.!
   A goal of the ${lgname} system
is to eventually have the classes partitioned
into system provided and user provided.
The operating system could then be a collection of resident classes
the user's application links with, including some of their own classes,
replacing any system classes seamlessly, as appropriate.
All programs would share a common pool for ${mss}.
Interprocess communication would be very straightforward since
a method invocation could just be packaged like an RPC (Remote
Process Call) call.
Right now there is no way to know exactly how many parameters
(or their byte widths) have been pushed on the stack.
.!
.!
.sect 1 4 Future: Extensible Classes
.!
  Dynamically extending the list of known classes is being
looked into.  This way a class could be paged in as needed, and
then expunged from memory when the system felt the class didn't
have any outstanding objects.
Ideally, the system should manage this without
.underline
any
management code in the user's application.
When running on different configurations, different classes might be
resident (ie, math coprocessor or software floating point).
.!
  And why not allow a class to extend itself dynamically?
Perhaps a friend could pass on a few methods she has added
to the
.class Sequence
class.
While the computer is up and running, the user would indicate
where the extension is stored.
Then one of your friends applications could be run, and the system
would automatically page in the class extension.
Class categories in version 4 are along this line...
.!
.!
.sect 1 5 Future: cpp replacement
.!
  Many nuisances could be eliminated if
.program cpp
were built-in to the translator.
All comments could be sucessfully parsed,
and error reporting could be improved by displaying the line as
the user entered it.
The translator might also run a little faster.
However, it would grow by over 20K, maybe more (and load much slower).
The replacement would be either from FSF or DECUS.
Since few symbols are inherent in UNIX
.program cpp,
this is no great loss.
.!
.!
.endsect
.!
.cl
.source biblo.rinc
.!
.! EOJ
